Steve Edge, the first league captain to be dumped after leading his team to a premiership, reckons there must be a good reason for stripping John Sutton of the South Sydney captaincy.
"I'm sure [coach] Michael Maguire would have discussed it with John Sutton and worked it out because you need a happy, harmonious family to be successful," Edge said.
"He did a great job. You don't know if it's good or bad, that decision, but you can never take away from him what he did. He certainly joined an elite group and he'd be proud as punch to captain a premiership-winning team.
"I'm sure Maguire would have given it a fair amount of thought and there would be a very good reason for it."
Sutton spoke for the first time about the decision to sack him in favour of Greg Inglis on Monday, but the reasons behind the shock move still are not apparent. Speaking at the launch of the NRL's Community Carnival, at which Olympic gold medallist Cathy Freeman was unveiled as an ambassador, a diplomatic Sutton would not reveal why he was axed just months after breaking the Rabbitohs' 43-year premiership drought.
In Edge's case, he began the 1978 season as skipper but relinquished the role to Dragons teammate Craig Young when dropped to reserve grade. Sutton's situation is different in that he is the first player in the 107-year history of the game not to at least begin a premiership defence with the "c" next to his name.
Advertisement
Whatever Maguire and Sutton discussed about the transition will remain between the pair.
"I wouldn't say difficult," Sutton said of the conversation.
"I guess we've had plenty of chats about it and I don't want to delve into what we talked about. I'm still very much a part of this team, I won't be going anywhere any time soon.
"The rumours had me going to the Tigers; I don't know where that came from.
"Like I said, I love this club and will be doing everything I can to get back to the GF."
Upon arriving at Redfern, Maguire appointed five co-captains. However, only Inglis has been chosen for the title defence. Asked if co-captaincy with Inglis was an option considered, Sutton said: "Coulda, woulda, shoulda – it was one of those ones. This is the way we've gone and I'm happy with the decision. It doesn't bother me one bit. I'm just all about getting the next win for Souths."
Sutton, who is contracted to the Bunnies for a further three seasons, is the club's most capped player with 229 appearances. It is unclear when he will make his 230th after undergoing shoulder surgery in the off-season. However, the 30-year-old said he was recovering well and was a chance of selection for the World Club Challenge in England.
"They thought I tore my labrum, they thought I needed stabilisation, which would have put me out for 12 weeks," he said.
"They got in there and it was all stable; I just needed a clean out. I had six bits of cartilage floating around and they cleaned it all out... Hopefully I can keep working hard at it and get on that plane."
