Landlocked Lebanon has the distinction of being the final resting place of a highly successful 19th century whaling captain, Sluman Lothrop Gray, who also managed to find time to serve a term in the state legislature.
But it's the saga surrounding his entombment in Lebanon's Liberty Hill cemetery that gives Gray a distinctive moment in history.
After he died at sea near the island of Guam, his remains were preserved in a barrel of alcoholic spirits so they could withstand the long voyage home to Lebanon.
Equally intriguing is his wife, Sarah Frisbie Gray, who some said killed most of her children. She was one of the first and most noted of the "petticoat whalers," wives of captains who sailed with their husbands, and often with their children, to keep their families intact.
Three of Sarah's eight children were born during whaling trips. But there remains to this day an air of mystery about this seafaring family from rural Connecticut.
Gray was born in Connecticut in 1813. His parents are unknown, as is where he was born and raised. His early years at sea were undocumented.
He was given command of his first whaling ship in 1842 at the unusually young age of 28 or 29. He became a wealthy man during the prime years of the whaling industry, and the owners of the vessels he commanded regarded him well enough to let him take his family along on five of his six recorded voyages.
Sluman Gray married Sarah A. Frisbie, of Canaan Township, Pa., in August 1838. A justice of the peace performed the ceremony in the tiny rural town of Columbia, Conn. Why they chose Columbia is another mystery. During the 1840s, they lived in New London, where Gray began to invest his earnings from his several profitable voyages.
Gray had the reputation of being a successful but foul-mouthed and tyrannical master. A cabin boy aboard the whaler Hannibal in 1849 transcribed some of his skipper's blasphemies in his journal. He also recorded that the crew was delighted when Sarah Gray told them her husband had taken gravely ill in his cabin. Their dancing did not last long, because Gray soon recovered and reasserted his command.
That brush with death, however, seems to have touched Gray. Whaling was a dangerous and arduous business. Voyages lasted until the ship's hold was filled with casks of oil and whalebone -- sometimes two, three or even five years. In 1851, Gray bought 10 acres in Lebanon and the family moved to town the next year. Their house in the Liberty Hill section of Lebanon still stands.
But after seven years ashore cultivating the life of a country gentleman, the sea called to Gray once again. He signed on as master of the New Bedford whaler James Maury. The ship sailed for the Pacific on June 1, 1864. On board were Sarah and their three children: Katie, 16; Sluman Jr., 10; and Nellie, 2. Only Nellie had not been to sea before.
"Captain Gray expired at 2 p.m. after an illness of two days," the first mate wrote in the ship's log on March 22, 1865. The James Maury was 400 miles off Guam. The next day, the ship's cooper made a cask for the captain. His body was sealed in the barrel after it was filled with spirits, most likely rum.
The voyage continued to the Bering Sea, where the confederate raider Shenandoah came upon the unarmed whaling fleet in June. News of Gen. Robert E. Lee's April surrender had not reached the ships. All but two of the whalers were burned by the confederates. The James Maury was spared because of the presence of Sarah Gray, her three children and the cask containing her husband's body.
They made it back to Lebanon in March 1866.
Sarah Gray's reputation has suffered ever since. David E. Philips, in his book "Legendary Connecticut," as much as accuses her of killing her eight children, five of them while her husband was at sea and she waited at home.
Not so, counters Lebanon historian Alicia Wayland, who has spent years investigating the Gray family and trying to find answers to the enigmas that surround them.
